<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kiautschou Bay concession</h1><p class="st-meta">1898 CE – 1914 CE · capital Tsingtao</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Germany's naval colony on the Shandong coast, centred on Tsingtao, whose transfer to Japan in 1919 triggered China's May Fourth Movement.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Using the murder of two German missionaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> in 1897 as a pretext, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozhou-bay">Kiautschou Bay</a> and in 1898 forced the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> to grant a ninety-nine-year lease, together with railway and mining rights in Shandong.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Uniquely among German colonies, it was run by the navy, with a naval officer as governor. Tsingtao was planned as a model colony, with modern harbour works, sewerage, schools, German-style architecture and a brewery founded in 1903, and the railway to Jinan opened in 1904. Chinese and European residential areas were kept separate.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, supported by British troops, besieged Tsingtao, which Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-meyer-waldeck">Alfred Meyer-Waldeck</a> surrendered in November 1914. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> gave German rights to Japan, provoking the May Fourth protests of 1919; Japan returned the territory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1922.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kiautschou-bay-concession.jpg" alt="Kiautschou Bay concession" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Imperial Colonial Office of the German Empire Vector: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reichskolonialflagge.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1898 CE – 1914 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tsingtao</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Oskar von Truppel, Alfred Meyer-Waldeck</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese folk religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mandarin, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kiautschou, Kiaochow, Tsingtao</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1906&amp;focus=kiautschou-bay-concession" aria-label="Kiautschou Bay concession on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7709 874 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kiautschou Bay concession lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8029 1130L5300 622M8029 1130L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5300 622h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8029 1130h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8029 1130h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1906&amp;focus=kiautschou-bay-concession">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

